title: No request timeout and no way to set one - a stalled server never resolves
  the Future
updated: 2026-10-13T19:50:24Z
---

`_http` (`lib/Net/Async/Forgejo/API.pm:77-86`) builds `Net::Async::HTTP` with only `user_agent` and `max_connections_per_host`. Net::Async::HTTP applies no timeout unless one is given, so a request to an instance that accepts the connection and then stalls stays pending forever. The sync sibling has `WWW::Forgejo::LWPIO->timeout` (default 30 s).

There is also no way to pass one in: `configure` (`API.pm:21-28`) knows only `url` and `token`.

    Net::Async::Forgejo::API->new(url => 'http://x', token => 't', timeout => 5)
    # Unrecognised configuration keys for Net::Async::Forgejo::API - timeout

The same goes for the transport itself: no `http` attribute to inject a preconfigured or mock Net::Async::HTTP (the tests reach into `$self->{_http}`), unlike `Net::Async::MikroTik`, which has `timeout`, `verify_ssl` and `http`.

Done when: `timeout` is a configure key with the sync default (30) applied to the default transport, an `http` key accepts any object with `do_request`, both are documented, and a test proves a timeout fails the Future.

